That structure is given by $\text{Kantian responsibility}
\Longrightarrow
\text{responsibility only for acts freely chosen}.$ Once that principle is in view, the lecture’s tension becomes explicit: $\text{collective historical burden}
\stackrel{?}{=}
\text{moral responsibility}.$ The question mark must remain. Sandel is not claiming that Kant has already been refuted. He is showing that the lived moral intuition of collective responsibility presses against a framework organized around individual agency and chosen acts.

