p.src:
The pressure is first posed as $\text{identity shaped by nation, culture, history}
\stackrel{?}{\Longrightarrow}
\text{moral responsibility}.$ Then the possible Kantian objection is made sharper: $\text{later generation takes responsibility for earlier generation}
\Longrightarrow
\text{later generation becomes an instrument of the earlier}.$ This is an important turn in the lecture. The problem is no longer only whether Kant can understand inherited shame. It is whether intergenerational responsibility itself may violate a Kantian prohibition against using persons merely as means.

